<PageLayout
  title="Accessibility"
  summary="How this site is built to be usable by everyone, and how to report a problem."
  updated="2 October 2026"
>
  <p class="page-lead">
    We want everyone to be able to use this site, including people who use a keyboard, a screen
    reader, magnification or a phone.
  </p>

  <h2>What we have done</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Pages work with a keyboard, and a “Skip to content” link appears first.</li>
    <li>Text and backgrounds are chosen for good contrast.</li>
    <li>Pages can be zoomed and work on small screens without sideways scrolling.</li>
    <li>Headings and lists are marked up so screen readers can navigate them.</li>
    <li>The guides and law tracker can be read without JavaScript.</li>
    <li>Animations are reduced if your device asks for less motion.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Our aim</h2>
  <p>
    We aim to meet the <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/" rel="noopener">Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2</a>
    at level AA.
  </p>

  <h2>Known limitations</h2>
  <p>
    The site has not yet had an independent accessibility audit, so there may be problems we
    have not found. Some links lead to other websites, which we do not control.
  </p>

  <h2>Report a problem</h2>
  <p>
    If something on this site is hard to use, please tell us through the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>. Say which page you were on and what went wrong, and we
    will try to fix it.
  </p>
</PageLayout>
